RECORD 4 — the baseline control, and an AMENDMENT to record 2
written 2026-09-02, ambiguous band running, no ambiguous item scored
=============================================================================

WHY THIS AMENDMENT EXISTS
Record 1 listed "this is a property of the target, not a defect of M.A.P." as
something the development evidence could not rule out. That was wrong in a
specific way: THE CONTROL WAS AVAILABLE THE WHOLE TIME AND I DID NOT COMPUTE IT.
The random walk and GARCH are normal-tailed models fitted to the SAME 178
outcomes. If fat tails were a property of 5-day post-earnings returns, their
z-scores would show the same excess. Recording an untested alternative as
"cannot rule out" when a one-command test was sitting in the same module is a
weaker statement than the evidence supported.

The amendment predates any ambiguous-band result: 21 of 353 items are complete,
none scored, and no ambiguous forecast has been read.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A — DEVELOPMENT CONTROL. Descriptive, and explicitly POST-HOC.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The development half has been looked at repeatedly. This is a comparator run on
an already-examined sample; it is NOT a test, and no decision about M.A.P. is
being taken on it. n=178 for all three, nothing skipped.

                  RMS     MAD   ratio   cluster-robust CI   |z|>2.5   |z|>3   |z|>4
  expected      1.000   1.000  1.0000                          2.21    0.48    0.01
  M.A.P.        1.333   1.105  1.2064   [1.0135, 1.4372]*    11 (7blk) 7 (5blk)    2
  random_walk   0.959   0.910  1.0540   [0.9736, 1.1921]      6 (3blk) 1 (1blk)    0
  garch         0.890   0.842  1.0577   [0.9579, 1.1912]      3 (2blk) 1 (1blk)    0
  * the only CI excluding 1.0

  largest |z|:  M.A.P. 4.59 4.34 3.99 3.46 3.43
                random_walk 3.38 2.77 2.59 2.55 2.51
                garch       3.29 2.53 2.52 2.27 2.27

The comparison is NOT confounded by the baselines being wider overall (their RMS
is below 1, so both are slightly over-dispersed on average, GARCH markedly).
The tail RATIO is scale-invariant by construction -- RMS and MAD-scale both
divide by any common factor -- so 1.206 against 1.054 and 1.058 is a statement
about SHAPE, not about width.

READING: on development, the "property of the target" explanation does not
survive. The same outcomes, scored by two normal-tailed models, produce tail
behaviour close to Gaussian. The excess appears to be M.A.P.'s. Record 1's
"cannot rule out" on that point is superseded by this; the remaining caveats in
record 1 stand.

A FURTHER DEVELOPMENT OBSERVATION, NOT PRE-REGISTERED AND NOT PART OF THE TEST
BELOW. M.A.P.'s sigma is narrower than the random walk's on 70.8% of items,
median ratio 0.814. But on the ELEVEN |z|>2.5 items the median ratio is 0.541,
against 0.834 on the other 167. So M.A.P. is not uniformly too narrow -- it is
roughly half as wide as a trailing-volatility baseline precisely on the items
that moved. Correlation of log sigmas is 0.625, so it tracks cross-sectional
volatility partially and not enough.

If that holds up it matters for Phase 3, because a MIS-ALLOCATION of sigma
across items is neither a location error nor a scale error nor a kurtosis
problem, and none of ADR 0032's family addresses it -- including the Student-t
successor, which fattens every item's tails equally. This is one post-hoc
observation on a much-examined sample and is recorded as a hypothesis, not a
finding. It does not alter the pre-registered plan.

