PART B — AMENDMENT to record 2's ambiguous-band pre-registration
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Record 2 fixed three statistics on M.A.P. alone. They are now to be computed
IDENTICALLY FOR THE RANDOM WALK AND GARCH on the same ambiguous items:

  1. tail ratio RMS(z)/MAD-scale(z), cluster-robust 95% CI, moving-block
     bootstrap, 4000 draws, seed 20260813, 10-day blocks
  2. count of |z| > 2.5 with distinct-block spread
  3. count of |z| > 3.0 with distinct-block spread
  (|z| > 4 reported as a raw count for all three, as in Part A)

Baseline z is (outcome - prediction.mean) / prediction.sigma with mean = 0 by
construction. Everything else as record 2. Still three statistics; the addition
is two comparators, not a wider search.

INTERPRETATION, FIXED IN ADVANCE:

  ALL THREE HEAVY (each tail-ratio CI excluding 1.0, or each |z|>3 well above
  expectation over several blocks):
    the target carries jump risk that a normal-tailed 5-day model cannot
    represent. M.A.P.'s tails are then NOT a defect relative to the alternatives,
    and Student-t remains the right successor -- but for a DIFFERENT REASON than
    ADR 0032 assumed: not to fix a fault in M.A.P., but because the whole family
    of Gaussian predictive distributions is misspecified for this event window.
    That distinction is to be stated explicitly if it occurs, because it changes
    what the correction is claiming to do.

  M.A.P. HEAVY, BASELINES NOT (this is what development shows):
    the excess is M.A.P.'s. The forecaster states distributions too narrow for
    its own errors while trailing-volatility models on the same outcomes do not.
    Student-t is then a patch on M.A.P.'s dispersion rather than a correction for
    the target, and the sigma-allocation hypothesis of Part A becomes the more
    promising line -- to be pursued as new work, not folded into Phase 3
    silently.

  M.A.P. LIGHTER THAN A BASELINE (M.A.P.'s tail ratio below either baseline's,
  or its |z|>3 count below theirs):
    RECORD IT. It is informative and unexpected, and it would mean the
    development ordering does not survive a second sample. It would put the
    development tail excess back to the false positive that p=0.066 permits, and
    the whole thin-tails line -- Findings #44, ADR 0032's tail reasoning, and the
    Student-t pre-commitment -- would be withdrawn rather than reinterpreted.

The withdrawal conditions of record 2 are unchanged and still binding: if
M.A.P.'s own three statistics do not replicate, the thin-tails reading is
withdrawn and Student-t is not adopted, whatever the baselines do.
